**Current support versus accepted design.** The Alpha HTML builder currently emits routes for roots with authored runtime work or fragments and expects an artefact at the entry root. The accepted queued page design selects only roots with an explicit `$page` purpose. It keeps directory routes and replaces activity-based emission and the mandatory homepage with command-specific no-page rules.

### Directory-based routes

The module root's directory determines the HTML output path:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    src/@page.moth              -> index.html
    src/docs/@page.moth         -> docs/index.html
    src/docs/basics/@module.moth -> docs/basics/index.html
    src/@home.moth              -> index.html
    src/@api.moth               -> index.html
]

The root module maps to the project index when it declares a page. A nested
directory maps to a nested index route. The normal module-root filename
(`@page.moth`, `@home.moth`, `@api.moth`) has no effect on the route name.

An ordinary file cannot declare a page, and `index.moth` has no special meaning.
The page migration adds no route override, filename-derived route, page alias or
new redirect. Existing `page_url_style` and `redirect_index_html` policies stay
unchanged.

### Explicit pages

After the accepted cutover, a normal module root becomes an HTML artefact only
when it declares the root-only `$page` purpose:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $page
]

The root at `entry_root` may remain declaration-only while descendant roots
declare pages. Each page candidate comes from the existing normal-module root
inventory; the builder does not scan arbitrary `.moth` files. A metadata-only
page and a page whose metadata uses resources both produce a complete document.

### Command-specific no-page rules

The accepted command policy distinguishes checking from HTML output:

- Directory-project `check` accepts a valid project with zero pages. It still rejects authored runtime or direct output without a purpose.
- HTML application `build` and `dev` require at least one page anywhere below `entry_root`. They do not require a homepage at `entry_root`.
- Single-file `check` accepts a declaration-only synthetic root, but authored runtime or output still needs a purpose.
- Single-file HTML output requires `$page` on the explicitly selected synthetic root.

Do not fabricate `index.html` to satisfy development tooling. Serve descendant
routes normally. If a tool needs a launch page, choose the real homepage when
present or the lexicographically first normalised page route. That choice
doesn't create a redirect or route alias. Requests to an absent site root
retain ordinary missing-route behaviour.

### Builder ownership

The HTML builder owns route and artefact policy. The frontend remains
builder-neutral. The builder decides:

- which `$page` roots produce HTML artefacts
- how routes are derived
- how output files are organised
- whether to reject unsupported reachable features

Source stays target agnostic. The HTML builder may choose JavaScript, Wasm or
mixed output, but source cannot query or branch on that choice. Builder
packages expose stable capabilities rather than target identity.

### Output folders

The builder generates output folders and manages them through manifests.
Builder settings in `config.moth` own output paths. HTML defaults to `dev` for
development and `release` for release builds. Treat generated folders as build
artefacts, not source.

### Release versus development output

The default build writes to the dev folder. The `--release` flag writes to the
release output folder. The dev server serves from the dev folder.

### Experimental Wasm mode

In the experimental `--html-wasm` mode, the builder can emit a route folder
containing `index.html`, `page.js` and `page.wasm` instead of a single HTML
file with embedded JavaScript.

